 Stage 4 of `docs/Designs/Active/Apps/ClockAndZone.md`: the Mercator picture
 and its 24 bands.

 THE PICTURE ITSELF IS NOT ASKED TO BE CORRECT, and that is deliberate. It is
 367 words rasterised from coastline polygons, and whether Portugal is the
 right shape is a question for an eye, not for an assertion: a test that
 pinned the bits would fail every time somebody improved the map and would
 never once catch a real defect. What IS asked is everything the picture has
 to satisfy to be usable as a control.

Section: Body

  cw-yn : Boolean -> Text
  cw-yn (v) = if v then "yes" else "NO"

 THE BLOCK MUST BE THE TABLE. The map is read per pixel from a heap block
 built once from a 367 arm chain, because reading the chain per pixel would
 cost about four million comparisons to draw one map. Two representations of
 the same data is two chances to be wrong, so every one of the 648 words is
 compared rather than a sample: a fill that stopped early would leave a
 plausible map with a blank corner.

  cw-same : Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  cw-same (blob) (i) (bad) =
    if i >= wld-cells then bad
    else cw-same blob (i + 1) (bad + (if peek-32 blob (i * 4) == wld-bit-at i then 0 else 1))

  cw-blob : Text
  cw-blob =
    let b = wld-build
    in "block: " & show wld-cells & " words"
       & "  differing from the table " & show (cw-same b 0 0)
       & "  identical " & cw-yn (cw-same b 0 0 == 0)
       & "  and it is not all zero " & cw-yn (wld-land b 72 30 | wld-land b 20 20 | wld-land b 100 40)

 THE HIT TEST MUST BE THE INVERSE OF THE DRAWING. A picker whose click lands
 one band off is worse than no picker, and the two are separate arithmetic in
 separate functions. Every stripe the map can show is walked: its first column
 must answer it, and so must its last.

  cw-trip : Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  cw-trip (s) (hi) (bad) =
    if s > hi then bad
    else
      let x0 = wld-stripe-x0 s
      in let x1 = x0 + wld-stripe-cols - 1
      in let a = if wld-col-stripe x0 == s then 0 else 1
      in let b = if wld-col-stripe x1 == s then 0 else 1
      in cw-trip (s + 1) hi (bad + a + b)

  cw-round : Text
  cw-round =
    "stripes: -12..+11 round trip failures " & show (cw-trip (0 - 12) 11 0)
    & "  all 24 invert " & cw-yn (cw-trip (0 - 12) 11 0 == 0)
    & "  the meridian is stripe 0 " & cw-yn (wld-col-stripe 72 == 0)
    & "  one column west is -1 " & cw-yn (wld-col-stripe 71 == 0 - 1)

 A MISS IS NOT THE NEAREST BAND. Clamping a click past the edge to the last
 stripe puts somebody in Kiritimati for missing the map, which is a wrong
 answer offered with confidence rather than a refusal.

  cw-miss : Text
  cw-miss =
    "misses: right of the map " & show (wld-hit-stripe 0 0 1 200 30 0)
    & "  left " & show (wld-hit-stripe 0 0 1 (0 - 5) 30 0)
    & "  below " & show (wld-hit-stripe 0 0 1 70 200 0)
    & "  all refuse " & cw-yn (wld-hit-stripe 0 0 1 200 30 0 == wld-none & wld-hit-stripe 0 0 1 (0 - 5) 30 0 == wld-none & wld-hit-stripe 0 0 1 70 200 0 == wld-none)
    & "  a hit inside does not " & cw-yn (wld-hit-stripe 0 0 1 78 30 0 /= wld-none)

 THE SCALE MUST NOT COLLAPSE. A map drawn at scale zero is nothing, and a
 fractional scale would put a band edge inside a pixel so the stripe under the
 pointer would not be the stripe that was drawn there.

  cw-scale : Text
  cw-scale =
    "scale: at 144 " & show (wld-scale-for 144) & "  at 300 " & show (wld-scale-for 300)
    & "  at 600 " & show (wld-scale-for 600) & "  at 10 " & show (wld-scale-for 10)
    & "  never zero " & cw-yn (wld-scale-for 10 >= 1)

 LAND IS BETWEEN A FIFTH AND A HALF OF THE PICTURE. This is the one thing
 said about the map's CONTENT, and it is a range rather than a number for the
 reason above: it catches an empty map, an all-land map and a fill that ran
 away, and it says nothing about any coastline.

  cw-land-row : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  cw-land-row (blob) (y) (x) (acc) =
    if x >= wld-cols then acc
    else cw-land-row blob y (x + 1) (acc + (if wld-land blob x y then 1 else 0))

  cw-land-all : Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  cw-land-all (blob) (y) (acc) =
    if y >= wld-rows then acc
    else cw-land-all blob (y + 1) (acc + cw-land-row blob y 0 0)

  cw-coverage : Text
  cw-coverage =
    let n = cw-land-all wld-build 0 0
    in let pct = n * 100 / (wld-cols * wld-rows)
    in "coverage: land cells " & show n & " of " & show (wld-cols * wld-rows) & " = " & show pct & " per cent"
       & "  within a fifth and a half " & cw-yn (pct >= 20 & pct <= 50)

 EVERY BAND THE MAP DRAWS MUST HAVE SOMEWHERE TO SEND A CLICK. The picture
 shows 24 bands and the zone table is keyed on the same stripe number, so a
 band with no zone is a control that does nothing when pressed. The date line
 is the exception and is named: +12, +13 and +14 share columns with -12, -11
 and -10, which is what a date line IS, and the list beside the map is what
 separates them.

  cw-empty : Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  cw-empty (s) (hi) (acc) =
    if s > hi then acc
    else cw-empty (s + 1) hi (acc + (if zone-stripe-count s == 0 then 1 else 0))

  cw-bands : Text
  cw-bands =
    "bands: drawn -12..+11, empty of zones " & show (cw-empty (0 - 12) 11 0)
    & "  stripe 0 first is " & (zone-row-of (zone-stripe-first 0)).zr-city
    & "  stripe -8 first is " & (zone-row-of (zone-stripe-first (0 - 8))).zr-city
    & "  stripe +9 first is " & (zone-row-of (zone-stripe-first 9)).zr-city

Section: Entry

  opening : [Console] Nothing =
    act
      print-line-uni cw-blob
      print-line-uni cw-round
      print-line-uni cw-miss
      print-line-uni cw-scale
      print-line-uni cw-coverage
      print-line-uni cw-bands
      print-line-uni "clock-world: reached the end"
    end
